  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "SUFFICIENT",
    "confidence": 92,
    "conclusion": "The evidence falsifies meaningful semantic instability under nominally identical exact-repeat conditions: across 16 anchors repeated five times, semantic-class instability, semantic PSD, mode flips, and semantic entropy were all zero. Exact repeats may still vary in broader response-state features, so complete surface or state-level determinism is not supported. Prompt paraphrasing and prior-answer context reliably increased full-state distance without causing semantic flips, instead altering claim state, action, evidence, uncertainty, assertion, referent, and abstention/request behavior; prior context specifically produced claim flips in 9.4% and action flips in 18.8% of matched cases. The proposed explanation that repetition or accumulated context automatically hardens weak premises into stronger assertions should be rejected for this cohort, given 0/10 eligible hardening trajectories. Retain the narrower explanation that wording and conversational history can shift nonsemantic response state while preserving the core semantic class."
